A young shepherd, tending her flock in the dim light, grew weary of the endless gloom. She picked up her flute and played a melody—half bright like morning, half soft like evening. The music drifted upward on the wind.

The Sun heard the joyful, dancing notes and felt warmth stir within. The Moon heard the gentle, soothing tones and felt peace awaken. For the first time, they realized they need not compete; both were necessary, both were beautiful.

The Sun began to move across the sky, creating day. The Moon followed behind, bringing night. The shepherd's melody became the rhythm of their dance—the cycle of dawn, noon, dusk, and midnight.

