Myth: In ancient times, there lived a restless river named Kiran who flowed endlessly toward the sea, never stopping to rest. Above him arched the sky, patient and still, watching the world below.

One summer, a terrible drought came. Kiran's waters grew shallow and slow, and for the first time in his existence, he stopped moving. Terrified and still, he looked up at the sky and cried out: "I am dying! I have forgotten how to flow!"

The sky, moved by his despair, wept gentle tears. As raindrops fell, they showed Kiran something remarkable—each drop that touched his surface created perfect circles, rippling outward, teaching him that stillness could contain movement, and movement could arise from stillness.

"You gave me back my flow," Kiran said gratefully.

"And you give me form," the sky replied. "Without you to receive me, I am merely endless blue. Together, we complete the cycle."

